//
// A NULL ARM MATCHES A NULL SUBJECT OF ANY KIND (`@FR-N-Match`; loft#1417, loft#2230, loft#2427).  A `null`
// arm matches a heap subject, a plain enum subject and a nullable collection, each as any nullable is
// matched.  Each section keeps its own cases and receipt.
//
// ── 1417-a-null-arm-matches-a-heap-subject ──
//
// @FR-N-Match — `match e { null => …, x => … }` eliminates `t?` and binds the `t` arm.  The
// rule is stated for every t, and it held for a SCALAR and for the synthetic inline element
// only.  For a heap subject the arm-head parser reads a VARIANT name, `null` is a keyword and
// not one, so the first pass broke out of the arm loop and the run died on a brace — three
// "Expect token" errors naming nothing the author could act on (loft#1417).
//
// Absence is spelled differently in the two places, which is why one arm shape could not serve
// both.  An inline `__nullable<S>` element carries it as discriminant 0 and the arm is a
// discriminant test like any other; a HEAP subject — a local, a parameter, a return — carries
// it in the store pointer (`@FR-L-Null-Which`), so its arm asks `OpRefIsNull` and names no
// variant at all.  An arm may now carry its own test for exactly that reason.
//
// The `null` arm covers no VARIANT, so exhaustiveness is untouched: an absent subject is not a
// variant of anything, and a match without a null arm still answers null for one — which is
// what the last cell holds down.
//
// A BARE binding arm over a plain struct (`match s { null => -1, x => x.a }`) is refused with
// *"'x' does not match struct type S"* BY DESIGN: at the arm root a bare lowercase name is a
// refused misspelling on a struct or enum subject (`@FR-M-Unit`, matching-history D-match-22),
// and `x: _ => x.a` is the spelling that binds (`@FR-P-Cap`).  The struct cells below use a
// wildcard; the binding spelling is pinned in
// `rules/collections/Col-Lookup/a-keyed-lookup-is-nullable-and-a-miss-discharges-and-propagates-at-every-kind.loft`.
//
// @falsified-at: 4ac66f6c — interpret exit 1 -> 0 and native exit 1 -> 0.  The control build
//   does not compile the file at all (the arm loop breaks and the parse cascades), so no cell
//   runs and the assertion column is 0 on both sides; the value assertions are what make a
//   pass mean more than "it parsed".
//   HOLDS: assertions, leak, panic, free-refusal, expectations are equal on both trees.
//   Falsify prints only the channels that MOVE and requires HERE to be clean, so each of
//   those is clean on both. The native side was not scored in this sweep.
//
// ── 2230-a-null-arm-matches-a-plain-enum-subject ──
//
// @FR-N-Match — `match e { null => …, … }` eliminates `t?` for every t.  loft#1417 gave the
// `null` arm to a heap subject (`1417-a-null-arm-matches-a-heap-subject.loft`); a PLAIN enum
// — a value enum, no payload variants — still went to the arm-head parser, which reads a
// variant name, so `null` stopped the arm loop and the file was refused with
// "Expect token }" (loft#2230).
//
// A plain enum is one discriminant byte, and its absence is that byte: 255 for a written
// null, 0 for a field never set.  No variant takes either value, so the `null` arm asks the
// one absence test (`null_test`, `OpConvBoolFromEnum`), and a variant arm, which compares
// the byte against its own number, never matches a null.  Both encodings are cells below.
//
// Like the heap arm, the `null` arm covers no variant: a match without `_` must still name
// every variant (`2230-a-null-arm-covers-no-variant.loft`, a file of its own because a refused
// file runs none of its cells), and a match with no `null` arm still answers null for an
// absent subject.
//
// @falsified-at: 6184655a5 — interpret exit 1 -> 0, native exit 1 -> 0.  The control refuses
//   the file on the first `null` arm, so no cell runs there; the ten value assertions are what
//   make a pass here mean more than "it parsed".
//   HOLDS: assertions, leak, panic, refusals are equal on both trees.
//
// ── 2427-a-nullable-collection-is-matched-like-any-nullable ──
//
// `match e { null => …, x: _ => … }` eliminates `τ?` at EVERY τ (`@FR-N-Match`), and `x: _`
// binds the whole subject (`@FR-P-Cap` at the arm root) — a nullable VECTOR and a nullable
// KEYED collection included.  A vector's slice arms stand beside the null arm; a keyed
// collection has no positions, so it is matched as a whole.
//
// Before loft#2427 neither parsed: a vector subject read only `[…]`, `_` or a bare name as an
// arm head, so `null` and `x: _` broke out of the arm loop ("Expect token }"), and a keyed
// subject had no match path at all.  The keyed capture is a view of the subject's store,
// bound once ahead of the arms: bound inside its arm it was pre-inited to null there, and a
// keyed null-init mints a store the view overwrote — the leak gate is that cell's channel.
//
// The cells that can FAIL:
//   c1  a `vector<integer>?` with `null` and `x: _`
//   c2  `null` beside slice arms that cover every length
//   c3  `null` and a bare name over a `vector<text>?`
//   c4  `null` over a call's `vector<S>?`, slice arm then `_`
//   c5  a guard on the capture
//   c6  a `hash<K[k]>?`                         — and its capture leaked one store per match
//   c7  a `sorted<K[k]>?` field, bare name
//   c8  an `index<K[k]>?` captured and looked up by key
//
// The control, which a too-wide fix would move: c9, a dense vector subject with a `null`
// arm, which never matches.
//
// @falsified-at: 0e95172cd — interpret exit 1 -> 0, native exit 1 -> 0
//   ARMED: the suite's leak gate (`LOFT_LEAK_CHECK=1`) for c6, which the runner arms on every
//   run; the rest is ordinary assertions.
//   WITNESS: the control does not parse the file ("Expect token }") on both backends; with
//   the arm heads alone and not the keyed capture's binding, c6 leaks `hash<K[k]>×2`.
//   HOLDS: panic and free-refusal are clean on both trees and both backends; HERE passes
//   every assertion with no leak.
//
// CHANNEL: the compile (the file did not parse), the assertions, and the leak gate.

enum Tok { Fn { x: integer }, Id { x: integer } }
struct S { a: integer }

fn a_null_arm_matches_an_absent_struct_enum() {
  t: Tok? = null;
  r = match t { null => -1, Id { x } => x, Fn { x } => 100 + x };
  assert_eq("{r}", "-1", "the null arm is taken for an absent subject");
}

fn a_present_struct_enum_still_reaches_its_variant() {
  u: Tok? = Id { x: 7 };
  a = match u { null => -1, Id { x } => x, Fn { x } => 100 + x };
  assert_eq("{a}", "7", "the Id arm is taken");

  w: Tok? = Fn { x: 3 };
  b = match w { null => -1, Id { x } => x, Fn { x } => 100 + x };
  assert_eq("{b}", "103", "and the Fn arm for the other variant");
}

// A match with NO null arm is unchanged: an absent subject matches no variant and the match
// answers null, which is how the value was reachable before this arm existed.
fn a_match_without_a_null_arm_is_unchanged() {
  v: Tok? = Id { x: 2 };
  present = match v { Id { x } => x, Fn { x } => 100 + x };
  assert_eq("{present}", "2", "a present subject still dispatches");

  absent: Tok? = null;
  m = match absent { Id { x } => x, Fn { x } => 100 + x };
  assert_eq("{m ?? -1}", "-1", "and an absent one still answers null");
}

// A plain struct subject takes the null arm too; the other arm is a wildcard, because a
// binding arm over a struct is a separate refusal (see the header).
fn a_null_arm_matches_an_absent_struct() {
  s: S? = null;
  q = match s { null => -1, _ => 9 };
  assert_eq("{q}", "-1", "an absent struct takes the null arm");

  t: S? = S { a: 8 };
  p = match t { null => -1, _ => 9 };
  assert_eq("{p}", "9", "and a present one does not");
}

// The scalar spelling the rule always held for, as the control.
fn the_scalar_spelling_is_unchanged() {
  n: integer? = null;
  assert_eq("{match n { null => -1, x => x }}", "-1", "an absent scalar takes the null arm");
  m: integer? = 5;
  assert_eq("{match m { null => -1, x => x }}", "5", "and a present one binds");
}

enum C { A, B, D }
struct H { c: C?, k: integer }

fn first(e: C?) -> text { match e { null => "n", A => "a", B => "b", D => "d" } }
fn last(e: C?) -> text { match e { A => "a", B => "b", D => "d", null => "n" } }
fn mk(i: integer) -> C? { if i == 0 { null } else if i == 1 { A } else { D } }

fn a_null_arm_matches_an_absent_vector_element() {
  v: vector<C?> = [null, B, null, A, D];
  out = "";
  for e in v {
    out += match e { null => "n", A => "1", B => "2", D => "3" };
  }
  assert_eq(out, "n2n13", "each null element takes the null arm, each variant its own");
}

fn the_null_arm_may_come_last() {
  assert_eq("{last(null)}{last(B)}{last(D)}", "nbd", "a null arm after the variants");
}

fn a_null_arm_beside_a_wildcard() {
  r = "";
  ev: vector<C?> = [null, B, A];
  for x in ev {
    r += match x { null => "n", B => "b", _ => "w" };
  }
  assert_eq(r, "nbw", "null is not swallowed by the wildcard");
}

// The two absent bytes: a field never written (0) and one written null (255).
fn both_absent_encodings_take_the_null_arm() {
  h = H { k: 7 };
  assert_eq(first(h.c), "n", "a field never set");
  h.c = null;
  assert_eq(first(h.c), "n", "a field set to null");
  h.c = B;
  assert_eq(first(h.c), "b", "a field set to a variant");
}

fn a_call_result_subject() {
  s = "";
  for i in 0..3 {
    s += match mk(i) { null => "n", A => "a", _ => "o" };
  }
  assert_eq(s, "nao", "null, A, then D through the wildcard");
}

fn a_binding_and_a_guard_beside_the_null_arm() {
  b = "";
  ev: vector<C?> = [null, B];
  for x in ev {
    b += match x { null => "n", y: _ => "x{y}" };
  }
  assert_eq(b, "nxB", "the binding arm binds the present value");
  g = "";
  ev: vector<C?> = [A, null, D];
  for x in ev {
    g += match x { null => "n", A if len(g) == 0 => "ag", A => "a", _ => "o" };
  }
  assert_eq(g, "agno", "the guard is asked only for a present A");
}

fn an_integer_result() {
  t = 0;
  ev: vector<C?> = [null, B];
  for x in ev {
    t += match x { null => 100, A => 1, B => 2, D => 3 };
  }
  assert_eq(t, 102, "null arm and a variant arm, summed");
}

fn a_statement_match() {
  st = "";
  e: C? = null;
  match e { null => st += "S", A => st += "T", _ => st += "U" }
  assert_eq(st, "S", "the null arm runs as a statement");
}

// A subject that cannot be absent accepts the arm and never takes it, as a scalar does.
fn a_non_nullable_subject() {
  r = "";
  ev: vector<C> = [A, B];
  for x in ev {
    r += match x { null => "n", A => "a", B => "b", D => "d" };
  }
  assert_eq(r, "ab", "the null arm is never taken");
}

fn s2_a_match_without_a_null_arm_is_unchanged() {
  absent: C? = null;
  m = match absent { A => 1, B => 2, D => 3 };
  assert_eq("{m ?? -1}", "-1", "an absent subject still answers null");
}

struct S3S { n: integer }
struct K { k: integer }
struct KV { k: integer, v: integer }
struct O { s: sorted<K[k]>? }

fn c1(s: vector<integer>?) -> integer { match s { null => -1, x: _ => len(x) } }
fn c2(s: vector<integer>?) -> integer { match s { null => -1, [] => 0, [a, ..r] => a + len(r) } }
fn c3(s: vector<text>?) -> text { match s { null => "none", x => "{len(x)}" } }
fn s3_mk(b: boolean) -> vector<S3S>? { if b { [S3S { n: 9 }] } else { null } }
fn c4(b: boolean) -> integer { match s3_mk(b) { null => -1, [h, .._] => h.n, _ => -2 } }
fn c5(s: vector<integer>?) -> text { match s { null => "none", x: _ if len(x) > 2 => "big", _ => "small" } }
fn c6(s: hash<K[k]>?) -> integer { match s { null => -1, x: _ => len(x) } }
fn c7(o: O) -> text { match o.s { null => "none", x => "{len(x)}" } }
fn c8(s: index<KV[k]>?) -> integer { match s { null => -1, x: _ => x[2].v ?? -2 } }
fn c9(s: vector<integer>) -> integer { match s { null => -1, x: _ => len(x) } }

fn test_a_nullable_vector() {
  a: vector<integer>? = null;
  e: vector<integer> = [];
  assert(c1(a) == -1 && c1([1, 2, 3]) == 3, "c1");
  assert(c2(a) == -1 && c2(e) == 0 && c2([5, 1, 1]) == 7, "c2");
  t: vector<text>? = null;
  assert(c3(t) == "none" && c3(["p", "q"]) == "2", "c3");
  assert(c4(false) == -1 && c4(true) == 9, "c4");
  assert(c5([1, 2, 3]) == "big" && c5([1]) == "small" && c5(a) == "none", "c5");
  assert(c9([4, 5]) == 2, "c9");
}

fn test_a_nullable_keyed_collection() {
  a: hash<K[k]>? = null;
  h: hash<K[k]> = [K { k: 1 }, K { k: 2 }];
  for i in 0..3 { assert(c6(a) == -1 && c6(h) == 2, "c6"); }
  assert(c7(O { s: null }) == "none" && c7(O { s: [K { k: 3 }, K { k: 1 }, K { k: 2 }] }) == "3", "c7");
  i: index<KV[k]> = [KV { k: 1, v: 10 }, KV { k: 2, v: 20 }];
  n: index<KV[k]>? = null;
  assert(c8(i) == 20 && c8(n) == -1, "c8");
}
